[{"id":"VRCE-2-37-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":1,"english":"So long as he could see the dust raised by the car of Rāma setting out for the forest, so long that best of the Ikşvāku race did not turn his eyes from that direction.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bcdc379b87f8d361a2f92bf2dc8e7c51d1de389e123e9d463b5a30624420dc75","compact":"cf8eeeee3f732a6dc7e6e1efdcf494b3ca363c7a68489e924bfb155f302ff299","nasal":"cf8eeeee3f732a6dc7e6e1efdcf494b3ca363c7a68489e924bfb155f302ff299"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":2,"english":"And so long as the king could discover his exceedingly virtuous and favourite son, so long he raised himself (on his toes) on the earth with the view of beholding him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fea429106ce77f6e0c8bb5cb0a8a73491a57bacb164429c5b2e8a4a4fd93c835","compact":"3724db2511aa3c12a07531a1ab2d1d99d487bf62df110de84b212829c7639226","nasal":"5ee2e6f4c2335db368c59021e8c9edf85440908979dc8eb6e732b5f5666eda97"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":3,"english":"When the ruler of earth could no longer perceive even the dust raised by Rāma's car, then pierced with sorrow, and in heaviness of heart, he fell down to the ground.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.849,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037003","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1b1e9835be9991d4dfc89016f346b4c22c1ef94c3cdbc3a5b6c2795de13e331f","compact":"fedb9672298b21bafa00e20537ffb9376dcffae2f52a2f6d54508d05b803daa5","nasal":"ca1a501acef822e6b1638ffd1199acbfa4d562502c3ba319049144ae80bdcad5"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-4","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":4,"english":"Then (raising him up), Kausalyā held his right arm and walked with him, while the slenderwaisted Kaikeyi walked by his left.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.515,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037004","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6a00eba22650c5d81644b4bf95509f36e17dc54c7032c3576a08a2e8e1ccf68e","compact":"2b7b7a50c8659aafae1c3061a479ed34cad7c383b6a3df1824a916c0977e07e3","nasal":"decc669936425f709f4bf6db8e9b88783080ca48d34ea2e884fd4e5d46f69110"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-5","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":5,"english":"Endowed with a sense of justice and with virtue and humility, the king with afflicted senses steadily eyeing Kaikeyī, thus spoke to her.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.695,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037005","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9e4fbba19b84aafe2a727dfe2a2f7ed610dd3c95c4a6e5c8e2d9d274328f5642","compact":"c181edab64b314f579cd7df81fcac882fd5d44335bbe0a7ca879aa8b9ae9940d","nasal":"fffc80d3d9413999a08c55f8e386e58f007d75c6f2f7ccfc40a2a74862043460"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-6","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":6,"english":"O Kaikeyī, that have decided for following sin, do you not touch my person, nor do I wish to see you. You are no wife of mine, not even a maid-servant of a friend sharing his good graces. I am none to those that subsist on your favour, nor are they anything to me. I renounce you who solely seek your interest and have abandoned virtue.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.447,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"12879e60cb39a30f48876219111613f3adc1c3bcd5fd98a56e5a63c2384b811b","compact":"13e7462e8bebab22df21a59882e25966ee3b1db8005d769e173399679e374d3e","nasal":"9fa3dec0d73bd9c8a60c809e863718926b3d71e2fdb5b19b5d19fc0b17a9b5a2"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":7,"english":"O Kaikeyī, that have decided for following sin, do you not touch my person, nor do I wish to see you. You are no wife of mine, not even a maid-servant of a friend sharing his good graces. I am none to those that subsist on your favour, nor are they anything to me. I renounce you who solely seek your interest and have abandoned virtue.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.864,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fe34488c6166ec7b07292c1be61cb2d8d672771d0814a4e616c4a1236cade956","compact":"8af1dadc1258d89eed30bb8706780bde030eee1fa243526e8fa0c8afd2d98d98","nasal":"068bd3f7ddf905374e778ce74cddc0683f653f603e9fb60d498c4132de41e301"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":8,"english":"I renounce all the advantages pertaining either to this world or the next which I am entitled to by virtue of having obtained your hand and having made you circumambulate the sacrificial fire.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.871,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ce597ddebd5f4b5894c9faa73272cdedbc800ad35a3c6da41b25c970cdafd804","compact":"c8a1901621300417d93b37b722688ea49ac8ae4f79eac368324d113b2113255e","nasal":"c8a1901621300417d93b37b722688ea49ac8ae4f79eac368324d113b2113255e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":9,"english":"If Bharata is satisfied with receiving this entire kingdom, let not what he spends on account of my funeral obsequies find its way to me.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.671,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cea824284aa5ff774e9e6338eae0c94fe7418f268a7df2abd5662b5a092e950a","compact":"f7668a696c17f1009f359100832406f241214c860608a2e0145ff6d538d081d4","nasal":"f7668a696c17f1009f359100832406f241214c860608a2e0145ff6d538d081d4"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":10,"english":"Then raising the lord of men covered with dust, the noble Kausalyā pierced with grief, stopped (along with the monarch.)","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.746,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"637d4a0399fbc72692a334130d20dc9d1adf9c7678c1b83024866d1959b3c3c1","compact":"4ac25037d686a86a883811704de3d1c7eb297d25f4bc89aa47d412f106ed7e7f","nasal":"4ac25037d686a86a883811704de3d1c7eb297d25f4bc89aa47d412f106ed7e7f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":11,"english":"The righteous one remembering Rāghava repented himself, as if he had slain a Brāhmaṇa through inordinate desire, or as if he had placed his hand in fire.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.921,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"09bb26eea55a0c6a666a6fe0e5e428fa422a7a75bce9cc110116295abd0961e9","compact":"4c2044030fa257a071e788981e1a000c55411b4f5cc151659336b14eec16b42f","nasal":"c08790259c5680d1adfb77dbcba0fead3fd018354b27852a0fb5d2e32784ee61"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":12,"english":"Having stopped again and again, the visage of the monarch lamenting on beholding the track of the car, appeared dim like the Moon invaded by Rāhu,","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.79,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"589334b7eea6933370b8d0f4bc6035073d6e545a4d38fa4870054075950261d1","compact":"6bb263e11f8082eef9c802ae094d0c1b8ffdc2bc29aaa34cec2444dee0df7a82","nasal":"6bb263e11f8082eef9c802ae094d0c1b8ffdc2bc29aaa34cec2444dee0df7a82"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":13,"english":"And stricken with grief, he lamented, remembering his beloved son, and thinking that by this time he had reached the precincts of the city, he broke out into the following.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.9,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3c4b58bb0b907666d4e45677bca2ade7f6e2ee62d0e8ca26009d21b6d09f344c","compact":"ec156f769fbaffc2481f646ad8042fbf51fac4f8b96eab033c72ebaedcd35cac","nasal":"5b701d44685deb702978fc9c838678fadcba8e057944eb2793575ce6c07d2c2b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":14,"english":"On the way are traced the foot-prints of those foremost of bearers that are carrying my son away; but that magnanimous one I do not find.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"29fa6fbc449d2f78d52eab8f1e9f8432674643bb525a1b34e3cc3512fca5c09b","compact":"e0851bcc3e64b192f6aa8cc99ad3bc0937aba0654d83a4c2a325237b6368982d","nasal":"6d6fe74d1bddcafd31a818518bbc3f91e44dfb2a7dc40e28f07e3dad25a4f8d2"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":15,"english":"And that meritorious son of mine, who, daubed with sandal, used to rest his head pleasantly upon a pillow, fanned by beauteous damsels decked in ornaments, will to-day surely take refuge underneath a tree, and lay his head on a wooden plank or a stone.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"837ba929847934682c5a75450bc11b84da3be9b9206dd421da946bad79ecf565","compact":"6dd66f1cff365279b96030e82d3b75865a1888c300930043a88c2ddb617ed872","nasal":"6dd66f1cff365279b96030e82d3b75865a1888c300930043a88c2ddb617ed872"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":16,"english":"Covered with dust, he heaving sighs will rise from the ground in sad guise, like a leader of she-elephants rising from the side of a mountain.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.882,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"483736c91ca18df55699319082fb1625c80c0f0762109cd736d4c8f00a00b969","compact":"7a62e2f91299a1dbe318758a09e2d08e5a4038bbb1e6ca7349fc723d98d476d1","nasal":"d7f8dbd87efb464dba7e170c18eee23aefa767714e8d0f277c851c84d50ad01b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":17,"english":"The rangers of the woods will now see the long-armed Rāma resembling the lord himself of the worlds, rising from the ground and going like one forlorn.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7d9b7bee36751ebf09195cfa8b7b50487a5a35be581caf3e74159fd42ff649b0","compact":"4e2e1b4e0ba5b7e2ee5c24731bd0cca6c3f235b60e30e1578674c31edb0613d8","nasal":"c4995ddbccf886a8a12c9f59d97df0af0b7be77a3cf6f17d6d9dc4fb73b2a3f1"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":18,"english":"O Kaikeyi, do you realise your desire, do you becoming a widow, rule this kingdom. Without that best of men I cannot live.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9784c86b6813f4a746f4baee547c99717d8c820aff557a72bebedbece7736f05","compact":"12a38a8d2c1d1a11d7dee42a06055893c64c336d48653f4be4f98ce8aa918601","nasal":"12a38a8d2c1d1a11d7dee42a06055893c64c336d48653f4be4f98ce8aa918601"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":19,"english":"Thus lamenting, the king surrounded by the multitude, like one that had performed his bath after death, entered that best of cities filled with people enfeebled and smitten with grief, with its streets thinned of men and its stalls closed.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.831,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a515dddd0bc37b911bde68c13deeaeea3c7f137ddca02de6d7b245a5d716f1a9","compact":"3e414109ec0f733dfeea63f4642c7b681c3116e90bbf8adebbe9fbd56881f701","nasal":"3e414109ec0f733dfeea63f4642c7b681c3116e90bbf8adebbe9fbd56881f701"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-20","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":20,"english":"And beholding that entire city, with his mind fixed upon Rāma, the king lamenting, like to the sun entering clouds, entered that city like to an unagitated sea rid of serpents by Suparna,* the city without Rāma or Lakşmaņa or Sītā. *Fair-feathered, a name of Garuda.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.718,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037020","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3fe6881557708ada7337ba6250768475dd7df35d835a9869493e97768cf0f1ca","compact":"619d5f5c8a31a3f9cc6041d9ab0e49820009ff1420eac1bed1c69ca79ca8611b","nasal":"5d7975160a4cd6f1087ed54bd0ab3b70f49af7044bb0cdbe7ad58f1b132c7898"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-21","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":21,"english":"And beholding that entire city, with his mind fixed upon Rāma, the king lamenting, like to the sun entering clouds, entered that city like to an unagitated sea rid of serpents by Suparna,* the city without Rāma or Lakşmaņa or Sītā. *Fair-feathered, a name of Garuda.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037021","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f00c68ac15e986a6759c8f3638aed100cbc7c7c34e8d3cdbd23aa0070c65c945","compact":"1a8421d60a3fdcdb3317f60f03b7bfdc7567e06e6c1ff8b59dfc40997f1fe752","nasal":"2330d7bf57538d3188a1304c6ce237c6e09d2ac2c8c0b9449ec7027a6f2eb92b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-22","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":22,"english":"And beholding that entire city, with his mind fixed upon Rāma, the king lamenting, like to the sun entering clouds, entered that city like to an unagitated sea rid of serpents by Suparna,* the city without Rāma or Lakşmaņa or Sītā. *Fair-feathered, a name of Garuda.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.902,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037022","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"198a932ffd98236e97a3b7e001e0c34360e7b0467c586ebdfd08c0f8727834db","compact":"ece454643ad93a4bc06ac0a2c8c649fbf7a8c40c433fe546d058645b2eb591a8","nasal":"ece454643ad93a4bc06ac0a2c8c649fbf7a8c40c433fe546d058645b2eb591a8"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-23","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":23,"english":"Do you speedily take me to the room of Rāma's mother, Kausalyā; for in no other place shall I find rest for my heart. When the king had spoken thus, the ushers taking him to Kausalyā's chamber, made his lie down in lowly plight.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.477,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-23","DUTT-2-42-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037023","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4c737a9acbd5aaa45bfd271c35a8d4b14d8fc0dbe5aa5ddf55c1ec5628777702","compact":"70bfa80671dce8e3c966abb714cafa5312b754c21cdfc909cd45a5499850e3be","nasal":"4d890d5420dc2b7006ce4d1d2a505f256cfb88460919754d1f984561a020c68a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-24","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":24,"english":"Having entered Kausalyā's apartment, the king having laid himself on the bed, was overwhelmed with emotion.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037024","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4d12fa9179cd72acc433ae4a3fb1d290110f972ce9da54e5e1792fd62619ac3d","compact":"373004cb23145f252e164b1d845e924c91d2b49e0f165341ecae4fe09368950e","nasal":"373004cb23145f252e164b1d845e924c91d2b49e0f165341ecae4fe09368950e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-25","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":25,"english":"Beholding this, the puissant sovereign raising up his arm, burst out into lamentations, saying, “Ah! Rāma, you forsakes us both! Ah me! surely those blessed people are happy, who having passed this gap of time, will behold Rāma returned and will embrace him.\"","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.667,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-27"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037025","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5199643d9a8e7e3eee5cf4dea30db3d323c5d7692fe050bf09430838fb6bb860","compact":"d88c737a291ee3eaf8bdf29086ed93ec08bfd075451e476ab982639804c8da21","nasal":"d88c737a291ee3eaf8bdf29086ed93ec08bfd075451e476ab982639804c8da21"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-26","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":37,"verse":26,"english":"Beholding this, the puissant sovereign raising up his arm, burst out into lamentations, saying, “Ah! Rāma, you forsakes us both! Ah me! surely those blessed people are happy, who having passed this gap of time, will behold Rāma returned and will embrace him.\"","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-42-27"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2037026","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d4fa01c3750b603b7d0442a0b5e77bddcf553fe49d0f352a832efab9560fbb43","compact":"19b93124658c795a94d8ab8d67e518620c408d64ba56845a7067e76cf60a6775","nasal":"5382dea263c42faf86295c326b02090aad7af61e9ed6e7fe1bb071d261f9ffad"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-37-27","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga"